// The demodulators, selected by a specialization constant.
//
// Written from published mathematics and published channel plans only, per
// docs/clean-room.md. The envelope, discriminator and product detectors are
// textbook (Carlson, "Communication Systems", chapters 4 and 5; Oppenheim and
// Schafer, "Discrete-Time Signal Processing", chapter 2 for the analytic
// signal argument that makes a real part a product detector at complex
// baseband). The deviation-to-bandwidth ratios quoted below are channel
// plans, cited where they are used. No GPL implementation was read, fetched
// or consulted while writing this file.
//
// Input is this receiver's complex baseband out of core/shaders/vrx_fine.comp,
// already mixed to DC, already limited to the requested bandwidth and already
// resampled to the demodulation rate Fd. Output is real audio at Fa = Fd / R,
// except for the raw tap, which hands out the complex baseband unchanged.
//
// WHAT THE MODE CONSTANT ACTUALLY SELECTS.
//
// kMode is the value of revenant::engine::Demod, so there is no mapping table
// between the enum and the pipeline and nothing to get out of step. Each
// pipeline folds to one mode at creation and every other branch disappears,
// which is why a switch that looks expensive here costs nothing at runtime.
//
// Four of the eight modes reach the same line. USB, LSB, DSB and CW are all a
// product detector, which at complex baseband is a real part and nothing else.
// What distinguishes them is entirely upstream: USB and LSB are an asymmetric
// passband, CW is a translation that puts the carrier on the operator's pitch,
// DSB is the symmetric case. core/shaders/vrx_fine.comp carries the table.
// Writing them as one line here rather than four is the honest description of
// what the arithmetic is.
//
// DETERMINISTIC TRANSCENDENTALS, WHICH ARE NOT OPTIONAL HERE.
//
// A demodulator needs a magnitude and an argument, and Vulkan specifies
// neither tightly enough to referee. The precision guarantees are: OpFAdd,
// OpFSub and OpFMul correctly rounded, OpFDiv 2.5 units in the last place,
// Sqrt 3 ULP, Atan 4096 ULP. A kernel calling sqrt() and atan() therefore
// produces different bits on NVIDIA and on AMD, and no CPU twin can be
// bit-exact against either, so the project's central rule would be unenforced
// exactly where the arithmetic is hardest to eyeball.
//
// So this file builds sqrt, reciprocal and atan2 out of the three operations
// that ARE correctly rounded, plus integer bit manipulation, which is exact by
// definition. The seeds are the standard exponent-halving and
// exponent-negating bit tricks and the refinements are Newton iterations,
// which are multiplies and adds only:
//
//   rsqrt   r <- r * (1.5 - (a/2) * r * r)     converges quadratically
//   recip   r <- r * (2.0 - a * r)             converges quadratically
//   atan    an odd polynomial in t^2 on [0, 1]
//
// Four iterations of each saturate float32 from the seeds' ~3 percent and
// ~0.4 percent starting errors. The atan polynomial's coefficients were fitted
// by least squares against long-double atan over [0, 1] and its worst error
// evaluated in float32 is 9.6e-08 radian, which is the float evaluation floor
// rather than the fit's: atan(1) is 0.785 and 0.785 * 2^-23 is 9.4e-08.
//
// The point is not that these are faster or more accurate than the built-ins.
// They are neither. The point is that they are SPECIFIED: the twin in
// core/dsp/vrx_reference.cpp performs the same operations in the same order
// and gets the same bits, on every driver, which is the only way a
// demodulator can be held to the project's bit-exactness rule at all.
//
// AUDIO SCALING, STATED ONCE SO MODES ARE COMPARABLE.
//
// The convention: a unit-amplitude signal fully modulating its own mode
// produces audio that swings to exactly +/-1.0. The per-mode gain is computed
// on the host and arrives as one float, so the convention lives in one place
// (vrx_demod_gain in core/dsp/vrx_reference.cpp) rather than as a magic number
// per branch here.
//
//   Raw   passthrough, gain 1. Unit input is unit output.
//   AM    envelope with DC removed, gain 1. A carrier of amplitude 1 at 100
//         percent modulation gives audio +/-1. Note that such a signal has a
//         peak envelope of 2, so an AM transmission that must stay inside
//         full scale carries a 0.5 carrier and reaches -6 dBFS audio.
//   NFM   arg(z[m] * conj(z[m-1])) * Fd / (2*pi*deviation), so audio is +/-1
//   WFM   at peak deviation and is independent of signal amplitude, which is
//         what a limiting discriminator is for. Deviation is taken from the
//         granted passband's WIDTH, high minus low, by the channel plan the
//         mode belongs to: 5 kHz in 25 kHz for land mobile NFM, so deviation
//         = width/5; 75 kHz in 200 kHz for FM broadcast, so deviation =
//         3*width/8. The width and not the reach: a passband pulled off
//         centre carries the same deviation, because what a discriminator
//         sees is how far the instantaneous frequency swings and not where
//         the band sits. This used to be written against "the requested
//         bandwidth", which was the same number while a request could only
//         be one symmetric width.
//   USB   Re(z), gain 1. A unit-amplitude tone in the passband gives audio
//   LSB   +/-1, which is the same peak-envelope reference AM uses, so a
//   CW    voice peak at full scale sounds the same loudness in every one of
//         these modes and in AM. Measured: 0.99995 and 1.00000.
//   DSB   Re(z), gain 1, WITH A CAVEAT that is physics rather than an
//         implementation gap. USB, LSB and CW carry their audio as a rotating
//         phasor, so a constant phase offset moves the audio's phase and not
//         its amplitude. A double-sideband suppressed-carrier signal does
//         not: its complex envelope is real and in phase with a carrier that
//         is not being transmitted, so a product detector with no carrier
//         recovery scales the audio by cos(theta), where theta is the
//         residual phase between the receiver's oscillator and the
//         suppressed carrier. Here theta is fixed by the fine filter's group
//         delay times the mix frequency and by the NCO's phase, so it is
//         deterministic and tuning-dependent rather than noise, and the
//         measured 0.743 at theta = -0.733 rad is exactly cos(theta). Closing
//         that needs a carrier recovery loop, which is carried state and so
//         is not a demodulator kernel's business; it belongs above this
//         layer, and M1 does not have one.
//
// The CPU twin is reference_vrx_demod in core/dsp/vrx_reference.cpp, and it is
// bit-identical rather than close: the same Newton iterations, the same Horner
// order, the same ascending tap index.

#version 450

// Never hardcoded; specialized at pipeline creation. Id 0 is the project-wide
// convention, core/gpu/kernel.h kLocalSizeXConstantId.
layout(local_size_x_id = 0) in;

// revenant::engine::Demod's value. Not a private numbering: the twin static
// asserts that these eight constants equal the enumerators.
layout(constant_id = 1) const uint kMode = 2;  // Demod::Nfm

// Fd / Fa. One for every mode whose demodulation rate already is the audio
// rate, which is all of them except wideband FM, where the signal needs a few
// hundred kilohertz to exist in and the audio does not.
layout(constant_id = 2) const uint kDecimation = 1;

// Length of the audio decimation filter, applied to the DETECTOR output
// rather than to the complex baseband, because for FM the detector is where
// the bandwidth collapses from the channel width to the audio width. One tap
// with unit weight when kDecimation is 1, which folds the loop away.
layout(constant_id = 3) const uint kAudioTaps = 1;

// Length of the AM DC-removal window. Only the AM branch reads it.
layout(constant_id = 4) const uint kDcTaps = 256;

const uint kModeRaw = 0u;
const uint kModeAm = 1u;
const uint kModeNfm = 2u;
const uint kModeWfm = 3u;
const uint kModeUsb = 4u;
const uint kModeLsb = 5u;
const uint kModeDsb = 6u;
const uint kModeCw = 7u;

// Fitted against long-double atan over [0, 1] by least squares on the odd
// basis t*(c0 + c1*u + ... + c9*u^9), u = t*t. Worst float32 error 9.6e-08
// radian over the interval. core/dsp/vrx_reference.cpp declares the same ten
// decimal literals, and both sides round them to the same float.
const float kAtanC0 = 0.9999999998;
const float kAtanC1 = -0.3333331929;
const float kAtanC2 = 0.1999923872;
const float kAtanC3 = -0.1427217912;
const float kAtanC4 = 0.1099615976;
const float kAtanC5 = -0.0853800324;
const float kAtanC6 = 0.0603537785;
const float kAtanC7 = -0.0336503503;
const float kAtanC8 = 0.0122847765;
const float kAtanC9 = -0.0021090222;

// Rounded to float once, here and in the twin, from the same decimal.
const float kHalfPi = 1.57079632679489661923;
const float kPi = 3.14159265358979323846;

layout(std430, binding = 0) readonly buffer FineRing {
    vec2 values[];
} fine;

// The audio decimation taps first, kAudioTaps of them, then the AM DC-removal
// weights, kDcTaps of them. One buffer rather than two so a pipeline that
// needs neither still binds something valid; the host supplies a single unit
// tap and a single dummy weight in that case.
layout(std430, binding = 1) readonly buffer Weights {
    float values[];
} weights;

// Real audio, one float per sample, except for the raw tap which writes an
// interleaved complex pair. Linear rather than a ring: this is the buffer that
// leaves the device, and audio PCM crossing the bus is one of the three things
// core/engine/engine.h permits to come back.
layout(std430, binding = 2) writeonly buffer AudioOut {
    float values[];
} audio;

layout(push_constant) uniform Params {
    // Fine ring capacity minus one, a power of two minus one.
    uint in_mask;

    // Ring offset of the fine sample that audio output 0 detects at. History
    // below it is read by the detector and by the decimation filter, and the
    // host guarantees it is still live.
    uint in_offset;

    // Audio samples this dispatch produces. One invocation each.
    uint count;

    // The mode's audio scale. See the header comment: one number, computed on
    // the host from the deviation and the rate, so the convention is not
    // spread across eight branches here.
    float gain;
} params;

// 1 / sqrt(value) for value > 0, deterministically.
//
// Seed from the exponent-halving bit trick, then four Newton iterations of
// r <- r*(1.5 - (a/2)*r*r). The seed constant is the usual one for float32 and
// gives about 3.4 percent; the iterations take that to 0.17 percent, 4.4e-06,
// 2.9e-11 and then below float resolution. Every step is a correctly-rounded
// multiply or add, and the bit manipulation is exact, so two implementations
// running this sequence agree bit for bit.
//
// Valid for roughly 2^-100 to 2^100. Outside that the intermediate r*r
// overflows or falls into the denormals that this project flushes to zero. A
// receiver's |z|^2 lives near unity, so the bound is a statement about the
// domain rather than a limitation in use.
float det_rsqrt(float value) {
    const uint bits = floatBitsToUint(value);
    const float seed = uintBitsToFloat(0x5F375A86u - (bits >> 1u));

    precise float half_value = 0.5 * value;
    precise float r1 = seed * (1.5 - half_value * (seed * seed));
    precise float r2 = r1 * (1.5 - half_value * (r1 * r1));
    precise float r3 = r2 * (1.5 - half_value * (r2 * r2));
    precise float r4 = r3 * (1.5 - half_value * (r3 * r3));
    return r4;
}

// sqrt(value), deterministically, as value * rsqrt(value).
//
// The guard is written !(value > 0.0) rather than value <= 0.0 so that a NaN,
// for which every comparison is false, takes the zero branch instead of
// falling into the iteration and propagating.
float det_sqrt(float value) {
    if (!(value > 0.0)) {
        return 0.0;
    }
    precise float result = value * det_rsqrt(value);
    return result;
}

// 1 / value for value > 0, deterministically.
//
// Seed from the exponent-negating bit trick, then four Newton iterations of
// r <- r*(2 - a*r). Callers must have reduced to a positive argument; det_atan2
// below does that with abs and max before it divides, which is also what keeps
// the quotient inside [0, 1].
float det_recip(float value) {
    const uint bits = floatBitsToUint(value);
    const float seed = uintBitsToFloat(0x7EF311C3u - bits);

    precise float r1 = seed * (2.0 - value * seed);
    precise float r2 = r1 * (2.0 - value * r1);
    precise float r3 = r2 * (2.0 - value * r2);
    precise float r4 = r3 * (2.0 - value * r3);
    return r4;
}

// atan(t) for t in [0, 1]. Horner, descending, which is the order the twin
// also uses; a different association of the same polynomial is a different
// rounding.
float det_atan_unit(float t) {
    precise float u = t * t;
    precise float p = kAtanC9;
    p = p * u + kAtanC8;
    p = p * u + kAtanC7;
    p = p * u + kAtanC6;
    p = p * u + kAtanC5;
    p = p * u + kAtanC4;
    p = p * u + kAtanC3;
    p = p * u + kAtanC2;
    p = p * u + kAtanC1;
    p = p * u + kAtanC0;
    precise float result = t * p;
    return result;
}

// atan2(y, x) in (-pi, pi], deterministically.
//
// Octant reduction first, so the polynomial only ever sees [0, 1] and the one
// division is min over max, which cannot exceed one and cannot divide by zero
// unless both inputs are zero. abs, min and max are bit selections and
// comparisons, so they are exact.
//
// atan2(-0.0, x) for negative x returns +pi here where the IEEE function
// returns -pi. The signed zero is the only input on which the two disagree and
// nothing upstream produces one: the discriminator's argument is a product of
// two samples.
float det_atan2(float y, float x) {
    const float ay = abs(y);
    const float ax = abs(x);

    if (ay == 0.0 && ax == 0.0) {
        return 0.0;
    }

    const float numerator = min(ay, ax);
    const float denominator = max(ay, ax);

    precise float ratio = numerator * det_recip(denominator);
    float angle = det_atan_unit(ratio);

    // Reflect out of the lower half-octant, then out of the right half-plane.
    // Neither subtraction cancels: angle is in [0, pi/4] at the first and
    // [0, pi/2] at the second.
    if (ay > ax) {
        precise float reflected = kHalfPi - angle;
        angle = reflected;
    }
    if (x < 0.0) {
        precise float reflected = kPi - angle;
        angle = reflected;
    }

    return (y < 0.0) ? -angle : angle;
}

vec2 sample_at(uint index) {
    // The mask is the reduction, and the subtraction that produced index is
    // allowed to wrap modulo 2^32: the ring capacity is a power of two
    // dividing 2^32, so the mask names the slot the unwrapped arithmetic
    // would have.
    return fine.values[index & params.in_mask];
}

float magnitude_at(uint index) {
    const vec2 z = sample_at(index);
    precise float square = z.x * z.x + z.y * z.y;
    return det_sqrt(square);
}

// The detector, at the demodulation rate, before any audio decimation.
float detect(uint index) {
    if (kMode == kModeAm) {
        // Envelope with DC removed. The DC estimate is a fixed weighted mean
        // of the last kDcTaps envelope samples, which makes the removal a
        // plain FIR highpass: a pure function of the absolute index, with no
        // state carried between blocks and therefore usable for retroactive
        // decode. A one-pole highpass would be cheaper and would not be.
        //
        // The weights are a normalised Hann window rather than a boxcar. Both
        // null DC exactly, since both sum to one and the direct term is one,
        // but a boxcar's first sidelobe is -13 dB, which leaves up to 1.9 dB
        // of ripple in the audio passband just above the corner. Hann is
        // -31 dB and leaves about 0.25 dB. Same cost.
        //
        // The subtracted term is delayed by half the window while the direct
        // term is not, so the highpass is not linear phase. In the passband
        // the subtracted term is small and the group delay is very close to
        // zero, which is the behaviour wanted; the phase wobble is confined to
        // the corner region, which is the stopband.
        precise float dc = 0.0;
        for (uint w = 0u; w < kDcTaps; ++w) {
            precise float term = magnitude_at(index - w) * weights.values[kAudioTaps + w];
            dc = dc + term;
        }
        precise float detected = magnitude_at(index) - dc;
        return detected;
    }

    if (kMode == kModeNfm || kMode == kModeWfm) {
        // The phase advance between consecutive samples, which is the
        // instantaneous frequency times 2*pi/Fd. Computed as the argument of
        // z[m] * conj(z[m-1]) rather than as a difference of two arguments,
        // because the difference of two principal values needs an unwrap and
        // the product does not: the product's argument is already the
        // difference reduced into (-pi, pi], which is exactly the range a
        // discriminator wants.
        const vec2 z = sample_at(index);
        const vec2 previous = sample_at(index - 1u);

        precise float product_real = z.x * previous.x + z.y * previous.y;
        precise float product_imag = z.y * previous.x - z.x * previous.y;

        return det_atan2(product_imag, product_real);
    }

    if (kMode == kModeRaw) {
        // Unreachable: the raw tap is handled in main() because it writes two
        // components. Present so every mode has a defined value here.
        return sample_at(index).x;
    }

    // USB, LSB, DSB and CW. A product detector at complex baseband is a real
    // part; the mode's identity is the fine stage's passband and mix, not
    // anything here.
    return sample_at(index).x;
}

void main() {
    const uint i = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;

    if (i >= params.count) {
        return;
    }

    const uint index = params.in_offset + i * kDecimation;

    if (kMode == kModeRaw) {
        // The raw tap is not a demodulator. It hands out complex baseband at
        // the receiver's bandwidth, which is what lets external tooling and
        // the decoder framework attach before any decoder exists. See the
        // comment on Demod in core/engine/vrx.h.
        const vec2 z = sample_at(index);
        precise float raw_real = z.x * params.gain;
        precise float raw_imag = z.y * params.gain;
        audio.values[2u * i] = raw_real;
        audio.values[2u * i + 1u] = raw_imag;
        return;
    }

    // Audio decimation, applied after the detector. For FM that is the only
    // place it can go: the signal occupies the channel bandwidth and the
    // demodulated audio does not, so decimating before the discriminator
    // would throw away the signal and decimating after throws away nothing.
    // kAudioTaps is 1 with a unit weight when kDecimation is 1, and the
    // specialization constant folds the loop to a single pass.
    precise float accumulated = 0.0;
    for (uint t = 0u; t < kAudioTaps; ++t) {
        precise float term = detect(index - t) * weights.values[t];
        accumulated = accumulated + term;
    }

    precise float out_value = accumulated * params.gain;
    audio.values[i] = out_value;
}
